# verify/param_space.pict — this repository's input space, in Microsoft PICT syntax.
#
# cv-infra expands this into a pairwise (k=2) covering array and runs verify/sim.py
# once per row, `repeats` times. It reads names and values; what any of them MEAN is
# ours.
#
# AXIS NAME -> ARGV. Every axis becomes one flag: `drive_v: 0.2` is handed to the
# script as `--drive_v=0.2`, in the order declared here. So a name must match
# verify/sim.py's argparse exactly and must be CLI-flag-safe
# (^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$, and never `help`/`h`). Values travel as text — the
# script parses them.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE COMMITTED ARRAY. The covering array is derived from this file at
# every run, and each case is identified by a hash of its own axis assignment, so a
# baseline follows a case across commits only as long as its values are untouched.
# Editing a value (or a name) retires the old case and starts the new one without a
# baseline — a rename is a deliberate act, not a formatting change.
#
# PICT picks the CELL: it is discrete and combinatorial, so it answers "which
# combinations must be tried". "How much does one cell vary" is what `repeats`
# answers instead.

drive_v:   0.2, 0.4
drive_t:   5, 10
yaw_rate:  0.0, 0.3

# Turning eats the aisle. The warehouse lane the sample places the robot in is not
# long enough to arc for 10 s, so a long turning run only ever proves that a rack is
# where the map says it is.
IF [yaw_rate] = 0.3 THEN [drive_t] <= 5;
